{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head 4848533c8e1de3ee4c455096fcde2b08c61198e9 on repair/instrumentation-artifact-reanchor, pushed to origin. Artifact 27c29e35eb21cc55f21327c5fdaf1016916d4664 at docs.\n\nWHAT I DID. Re-derived the citation map at cbddb3d8 rather than trusting my earlier count, and found twenty-one pages resting on predecessors the merge superseded. They now cite the merged successors at the surviving directory paths. serve.md and architecture.md additionally gained the merged observe and telemetry artifacts, because both now describe observation.\n\nTHE JUDGEMENT YOUR CONDITIONS ASKED FOR, AND WHERE IT BIT. Replacing every citation mechanically failed the flare-distribution gate: the cmd/gs successor governed 13 of 47 pages against a limit of 11. I did not tune the number to get under the cap. The gate's own advice is to anchor over-covered pages to something narrower, and two of the thirteen had cmd/gs as an incidental basis rather than their subject. docs/reference/mcp/status.md documents the MCP surface and already carried internal/service, which is what implements it. docs/concepts/work-loop.md is about the fold's commitment semantics and carries internal/workroom. Both lost the cmd/gs basis instead of gaining a new one, which is more accurate about what those pages describe and happens to land the count at eleven. If you disagree with either, they are the two lines to argue with; the other nineteen are straight successions.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO, DELIBERATELY. I have not retired the predecessors yet. Retirement and re-anchoring have to land together or main is red in between, and this head is only half of that pair until it is approved and merged. Once it merges I will retire e517fd6e, c45e6fef, 61bafadb, 1555aaee and b7c2ffe5, which are mine, and verify each from projection.acts rather than from a batch count. 5644516f and 741cc094 are codex's and only codex can retire them.\n\nNote b7c2ffe5 at cmd/gs/main.go is not name